From 8d4312ff9503bc5efa1be3ff2245f28c94e86c3f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (draft)" <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 16:52:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] mm/folio: don't let one unresponsive CPU stall
 best-effort LRU drains

lru_add_drain_all() queues a work item on every CPU whose per-CPU LRU
caches are not empty and then flush_work()s each of them, all under a
global mutex. A CPU that cannot run its kworker for a while - a
hardirq storm from a broken device, a long irqs- or preempt-off
section, a runaway SCHED_FIFO task when the fair server is disabled,
a misbehaving sched_ext scheduler - therefore stalls not only the
caller but everyone who queues up behind it on the mutex.

Most callers only drain as an optimisation: khugepaged before each
scan pass, MADV_COLLAPSE, POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED, KSM, the last retry of
proactive memory.reclaim and drop_caches. For them a stale per-CPU
cache on one CPU only means a few folios are skipped this time.

Add lru_add_drain_all_bounded() for these callers. It gives up after
vm.lru_drain_timeout_ms (default 20 ms), both while waiting for the
mutex and while waiting for the per-CPU works, and counts that as
lru_drain_timeout in /proc/vmstat. Callers that need the drain for
correctness (migration via lru_cache_disable(), long-term GUP pins,
memfd sealing, compaction via sysctl, memory-failure, ...) keep the
old semantics, but if they wait for longer than a second the stuck
CPUs are now named in the kernel log and lru_drain_slow is counted.

To make that possible:
 - each per-CPU work records the drain generation it started under,
   so waiters can wait with a timeout instead of flush_work();
 - "a newer generation was scheduled" no longer implies "it has
   completed", so the early exit at (C) now checks a separate
   lru_drain_completed generation;
 - the work items are initialised once instead of on every round, as
   one left pending by a bounded drain must not be re-initialised;
 - waiters are only woken when somebody waits (wq_has_sleeper()),
   since every per-CPU drain work does it.

Tested in QEMU (2 vCPUs, 7.3.1 and 7.4-rc1 with PROVE_LOCKING), with a SCHED_FIFO
task faulting in pages on CPU1 and CPU1's fair server set to 0:
  drop_caches, lru_drain_timeout_ms=0:  first call 4974-5064 ms
  drop_caches, lru_drain_timeout_ms=20: every call 26-34 ms
  compact_memory (strict) with the hog:  ~4083 ms, CPU 1 reported
  drop_caches while compact_memory holds the mutex: 25-30 ms
With the fair server left at its default both settings behave the
same (no timeouts). No lockdep reports.

DRAFT for linux-lunar; not submitted upstream.

