+lru_drain_timeout_ms
+====================
+
+Some kernel paths flush the per-CPU LRU caches of every CPU by queueing a
+work item on each CPU and waiting for it to run. If a CPU cannot run its
+kworker for a while (for example because of an interrupt storm, a busy
+real-time task or a misbehaving driver), all of them wait for that CPU.
+
+For callers that only drain as an optimisation (khugepaged and
+MADV_COLLAPSE, POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED, KSM, proactive memory.reclaim and
+drop_caches) this sysctl bounds that wait, in milliseconds. If the time
+runs out they carry on without the remaining CPUs and the
+``lru_drain_timeout`` counter in /proc/vmstat is incremented; the
+remaining work items still run later. Callers that depend on the drain
+for correctness (page migration, long-term page pinning, contiguous
+allocations, memory hotplug, ...) always wait. If one of those waits for
+longer than a second, the stuck CPUs are reported in the kernel log and
+``lru_drain_slow`` is incremented.
+
+0 makes the bounded callers wait as long as necessary as well. The
+default is 20, the maximum 1000.
+
+
 max_map_count
